<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.14.3: Analyzing Functions Using Different Representations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01993</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.14.3: Analyzing Functions Using Different Representations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1a1c315e-480c-4ce2-be46-eee69427297d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Here is a graph that represents the height of a baseball, \(h\), in feet as a function of time, \(t\), in seconds after it was hit by Player A.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Horizontal axis, time in seconds. Vertical axis, height in feet. Parabola, opens down, x intercepts at about -0.62 and about 5.12, vertex = 2 point 5 comma 105."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/719350de85b749cac26a9fe76d9d4d9989353b8a"/></media></figure></para>
<para>The function \(g\) defined by \(g(t)=(-16t−1)(t−4)\) also represents the height in feet of a baseball \(t\) seconds after it was hit by Player B. Without graphing function \(g\), answer the following questions and explain or show how you know.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="ff1cdcd1-fdec-4cce-97cb-cbe299193eb5" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Which player's baseball stayed in flight longer?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Player A's baseball stayed in flight longer. The zeros of function \(g\) are 4 and \(-\frac{1}{16}\). The negative zero doesn't have any meaning in this situation. A zero in this situation means the time when the baseball has a height of 0 (or when it hits the ground). For Player B, this happened when \(t=4\) or 4 seconds after it was hit. Player A's baseball hit the ground a little over 5 seconds after it was hit.</para><para>Incorrect. The correct answer is: Player A's baseball stayed in flight longer. The zeros of function \(g\) are 4 and \(-\frac{1}{16}\). The negative zero doesn't have any meaning in this situation. A zero in this situation means the time when the baseball has a height of 0 (or when it hits the ground). For Player B, this happened when \(t=4\) or 4 seconds after it was hit. Player A's baseball hit the ground a little over 5 seconds after it was hit.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="9280e901-492a-4e45-94b3-e5a9d7d012a5" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Which player's baseball reached a greater maximum height?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Player A's baseball reached a greater maximum height. From the graph for Player A, it looks like the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is around 105 feet. For Player B, the vertex of the graph has a \(t\)-coordinate of about 2 (halfway between \(\frac{-1}{16}\) and 4). We can find \(g(2)\) to estimate the height at that point. \(g(2)=(-16(2)−1)(2−4)=(-33)(-2)=66\), so the highest point of Player B's baseball was around 66 feet.</para><para>The correct answer is: Player A's baseball reached a greater maximum height. From the graph for Player A, it looks like the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is around 105 feet. For Player B, the vertex of the graph has a \(t\)-coordinate of about 2 (halfway between \(\frac{-1}{16}\) and 4). We can find \(g(2)\) to estimate the height at that point. \(g(2)=(-16(2)−1)(2−4)=(-33)(-2)=66\), so the highest point of Player B's baseball was around 66 feet.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Based on the graph, what is the approximate height, in feet, of Player A’s baseball when hit? How do you know?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>We can look at the \(y\)-intercept of the graph, and it looks like the \(y\)-coordinate is about 5.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="41ccb94e-1b66-4125-afd1-e40694755a1a" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>What is the height, in feet, of Player B’s baseball when hit?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 4 feet. Find the height when \(t=0\). \(g(0)=(-16(0)−1)(0−4)=(-1)(-4)=4\), so it was hit at 4 feet.</para><para>The correct answer is 4 feet. Find the height when \(t=0\). \(g(0)=(-16(0)−1)(0−4)=(-1)(-4)=4\), so it was hit at 4 feet.</para></solution></exercise></section>

